WHAT: make drumstick-vpiano a wk node -- default to the `wk` QPA platform,
      statically import the three plugins wasm cannot dlopen, link libqwk.a,
      Qt6::FbSupportPrivate and BOTH component-type objects, compile a font in
      as a Qt resource, and stop the NET_BACKEND arm from firing on a sysroot
      that merely happens to contain QtNetwork.
WHY:  five things a wasm32-wasip2 Qt node needs that a desktop build gets free:
        * QT_QPA_PLATFORM. plugins/qt's qtbase was configured
          QT_QPA_DEFAULT_PLATFORM=offscreen (it predates the wk QPA plugin), so
          an unmodified app renders to nowhere and never opens a surface. Set
          only when the environment does not already say, so
          QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen stays available for bisecting failures.
        * Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN. No dlopen on wasm. Without QWkIntegrationPlugin,
          QApplication aborts with "no Qt platform plugin could be initialized"
          and an EMPTY plugin list -- it reads like a broken Qt, but it is an
          unreferenced symbol. Upstream's own #if defined(XXX_BACKEND) block
          already does exactly this for its five other static backends, so the
          WK_BACKEND arm is that pattern with our two names in it; the QPA line
          is the one addition, because the wk platform plugin is out of tree on
          purpose and Qt's deployment glue therefore never writes it.
        * the component-type objects must arrive as LINK OPTIONS. Each holds
          only a `component-type` custom section that wasm-component-ld reads
          to build the import list; nothing references them, so as archive
          members they are dropped and the binary ends up with no wasi:surface
          and no wk:midi import -- and wk then gives the node neither a surface
          nor MIDI ports. TWO of them in one link is what this port had to
          establish, and they merge into one world.
        * fonts. Qt 6 ships none and a wk node has no host font dir; with none
          the app runs, QFontDatabase is empty, and every string renders as
          nothing at all -- including every note name on every key.
        * NET_BACKEND. Upstream asks whether Qt6Network EXISTS, but
          drumstick-rt-net-in/out are only DECLARED when USE_NETWORK was on. A
          sysroot with a cross-built libQt6Network.a and USE_NETWORK=OFF -- 
          which is exactly plugins/qt/sysroot -- therefore links two targets
          that were never created. Keying off HAVE_NETWORK is the same test
          library/rt-backends/CMakeLists.txt already uses.
      Everything is guarded (`#if defined(WK_BACKEND)`, `if(WK_...)`,
      `MATCHES "WASI"`), so a desktop build of this tree is unaffected.
UPSTREAM: the NET_BACKEND hunk is as-is -- it is a real bug in their tree and
      not wk-specific. The rest is as-is in spirit: it is the standard static-Qt
      deployment shape, which upstream already performs for its own static
      builds; it is wk-specific only in naming OUR platform plugin and OUR
      backends.
diff --git a/utils/vpiano/CMakeLists.txt b/utils/vpiano/CMakeLists.txt
index 6f6fef3..7e0d796 100644
--- a/utils/vpiano/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/utils/vpiano/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -31,7 +31,19 @@ if(APPLE)
     set(EXTRA_LIBS ${CORELIBS})
 endif()
 
+# --- wk node: fonts ---------------------------------------------------------
+# Qt 6 ships no fonts and a wk node has no host font directory to fall back on:
+# with none, the app runs, QFontDatabase is empty, and every string renders as
+# nothing at all -- including the note names on the keys, which is most of what
+# this app draws. build.sh stages one TTF and generates this .qrc beside it
+# (which font exists depends on the machine); the wk QPA's font database falls
+# back to :/fonts after looking at QT_QPA_FONTDIR and the usual VFS
+# directories, so a node that bind-mounts a real font directory can still
+# override it and never touch this copy. AUTORCC compiles it in.
+set(WK_FONTS_QRC "" CACHE FILEPATH "generated .qrc holding one staged TTF under :/fonts")
+
 set(vpiano_SRCS
+    ${WK_FONTS_QRC}
     connections.cpp
     connections.h
     connections.ui
@@ -154,8 +166,16 @@ if(STATIC_DRUMSTICK)
             winmm)
     endif()
 
-    find_package(Qt${QT_VERSION_MAJOR}Network)
-    if(Qt${QT_VERSION_MAJOR}Network_FOUND)
+    # HAVE_NETWORK, not a fresh find_package. Upstream asks Qt6Network whether
+    # it EXISTS, but drumstick-rt-net-in/out are only built when USE_NETWORK
+    # was on (library/rt-backends/CMakeLists.txt gates them on HAVE_NETWORK) --
+    # so on a tree where QtNetwork happens to be installed but the backend was
+    # switched off, this links two targets that were never declared. That is
+    # not hypothetical here: plugins/qt/sysroot has grown a cross-built
+    # libQt6Network.a, and this build needs USE_NETWORK=OFF because the net
+    # backend is QUdpSocket-based and would want socket notifiers on a
+    # multicast socket.
+    if(HAVE_NETWORK)
         target_compile_definitions(drumstick-vpiano PUBLIC NET_BACKEND)
         target_link_libraries(drumstick-vpiano PRIVATE
             drumstick-rt-net-in
@@ -166,7 +186,60 @@ if(STATIC_DRUMSTICK)
         target_compile_definitions(drumstick-vpiano PUBLIC FLUIDSYNTH_BACKEND)
         target_link_libraries(drumstick-vpiano PRIVATE drumstick-rt-fluidsynth)
     endif()
+
+    # The wk backends: this node's one MIDI inbox and outbox. See
+    # library/rt-backends/wk-in and wk-out; WK_BACKEND is what turns on the
+    # matching Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN pair in vpianomain.cpp.
+    # STREQUAL, for the reason spelled out in
+    # library/rt-backends/CMakeLists.txt: `if(${CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME} MATCHES
+    # "WASI")` is silently FALSE, because Platform/WASI.cmake defines a
+    # variable named WASI and MATCHES dereferences its unquoted operand.
+    if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "WASI")
+        target_compile_definitions(drumstick-vpiano PUBLIC WK_BACKEND)
+        target_link_libraries(drumstick-vpiano PRIVATE
+            drumstick-rt-wk-in
+            drumstick-rt-wk-out)
+    endif()
+endif()
+
+# --- wk node: the platform plugin and the component's imports ---------------
+# There is no dlopen on wasm, so every Qt plugin is an ARCHIVE linked into the
+# binary and named once with Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN (see vpianomain.cpp).
+#
+#   WK_QPA_LIB               plugins/qt/sysroot/lib/libqwk.a -- the wk QPA
+#                            plugin. It also carries the gfx-compat shim
+#                            objects (wkgfx.c plus its wit-bindgen output) and,
+#                            on a recent enough build, the clipboard bridge, so
+#                            the app needs no wasi-gfx code of its own.
+#   WK_COMPONENT_TYPE_OBJS   the *_component_type.o objects, in a ;-list:
+#                            wkgfx (always), wkmidi (always), wkclipboard (only
+#                            when this libqwk.a references it -- build.sh
+#                            probes the archive rather than guessing).
+#
+# Those objects arrive as LINK OPTIONS and never as libraries. Each carries
+# only a `component-type` custom section that wasm-component-ld reads to build
+# the component's import list; nothing references them, so as archive members
+# the linker drops them and the result is a component with no wasi:surface and
+# no wk:midi import at all -- which wk then refuses to give either a surface or
+# MIDI ports. SEVERAL of them in one link is the thing this port needed to
+# establish, and they MERGE: the component ends up importing wasi:surface and
+# wk:midi/midi together.
+set(WK_QPA_LIB "" CACHE FILEPATH "libqwk.a, the wk QPA plugin (plugins/qt/build-qpa.sh)")
+set(WK_COMPONENT_TYPE_OBJS "" CACHE STRING "wit-bindgen *_component_type.o objects to link")
+if(WK_QPA_LIB)
+    # FbSupportPrivate is not optional and not obvious: the wk QPA plugin IS
+    # fbconvenience -- QWkScreen derives from QFbScreen, QWkWindow from
+    # QFbWindow, QWkBackingStore from QFbBackingStore -- and that is where the
+    # guest-side compositing of N top-level windows into one surface lives.
+    # Without it the link fails with a page of "undefined symbol:
+    # QFbScreen::..." from inside libqwk.a. It is an INTERNAL_MODULE, so it is
+    # its own package rather than a component of Qt6Gui.
+    find_package(Qt6 6.8 REQUIRED COMPONENTS FbSupportPrivate)
+    target_link_libraries(drumstick-vpiano PRIVATE "${WK_QPA_LIB}" Qt6::FbSupportPrivate)
 endif()
+foreach(obj IN LISTS WK_COMPONENT_TYPE_OBJS)
+    target_link_options(drumstick-vpiano PRIVATE "${obj}")
+endforeach()
 
 install(TARGETS drumstick-vpiano
         RUNTIME DESTINATION ${CMAKE_INSTALL_BINDIR}
diff --git a/utils/vpiano/vpianomain.cpp b/utils/vpiano/vpianomain.cpp
index c4194e7..4efba88 100644
--- a/utils/vpiano/vpianomain.cpp
+++ b/utils/vpiano/vpianomain.cpp
@@ -68,8 +68,34 @@ Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN(OSSInput)
 Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN(OSSOutput)
 #endif
 
+#if defined(WK_BACKEND)
+// This node's MIDI inbox and outbox (library/rt-backends/wk-in, wk-out), and
+// the platform plugin it draws through. The QPA one is not part of upstream's
+// pattern above because Qt's own deployment glue writes it for you -- but the
+// wk QPA is deliberately OUT OF TREE (plugins/qt/qpa), so nothing generates
+// it and it has to be named here. Without the line QApplication aborts with
+// "no Qt platform plugin could be initialized" and an EMPTY plugin list, which
+// reads like a broken Qt and is actually an unreferenced symbol.
+#include <QtPlugin>
+Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN(WkMIDIInput)
+Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN(WkMIDIOutput)
+Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN(QWkIntegrationPlugin)
+#endif
+
 int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 {
+#if defined(WK_BACKEND)
+    // plugins/qt's qtbase was configured QT_QPA_DEFAULT_PLATFORM=offscreen --
+    // it predates the wk QPA plugin -- so without this the app comes up on a
+    // platform that renders to nowhere and never opens a wk surface. An
+    // explicit QT_QPA_PLATFORM in the node's environment still wins, which is
+    // what makes `QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen` usable for separating "Qt is
+    // broken" from "the wk QPA is broken".
+    if (!qEnvironmentVariableIsSet("QT_QPA_PLATFORM")) {
+        qputenv("QT_QPA_PLATFORM", "wk");
+    }
+#endif
+
     QApplication app(argc, argv);
     QCoreApplication::setOrganizationName("drumstick.sourceforge.net");
     QCoreApplication::setOrganizationDomain("drumstick.sourceforge.net");
